1
00:00:01,250 --> 00:00:03,120
Somewhere off the coast of Oman,

2
00:00:03,180 --> 00:00:06,230
a ship as long as three football fields is waiting.

3
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:07,460
In its belly:

4
00:00:07,530 --> 00:00:09,343
two million barrels of oil.

5
00:00:10,523 --> 00:00:12,063
Ahead of it lies a gap of

6
00:00:12,083 --> 00:00:14,743
water only about thirty-three kilometres wide.

7
00:00:15,033 --> 00:00:16,759
Narrower than a marathon.

8
00:00:17,859 --> 00:00:18,919
Yet before this year,

9
00:00:19,029 --> 00:00:21,270
about one in every five barrels of oil

10
00:00:21,290 --> 00:00:24,182
the world burned passed through this one gap.

11
00:00:25,162 --> 00:00:26,662
And in twenty twenty-six,

12
00:00:26,772 --> 00:00:28,662
when the ships almost stopped coming,

13
00:00:28,822 --> 00:00:30,452
oil prices around the world shot

14
00:00:30,472 --> 00:00:32,038
up by more than half.

15
00:00:33,228 --> 00:00:34,498
By the end of this video,

16
00:00:34,518 --> 00:00:35,362
you'll know why one narrow strait

17
00:00:35,382 --> 00:00:36,138
can shake the whole world.

18
00:00:36,158 --> 00:00:40,286
And why, forty years ago, it didn't.

19
00:00:41,186 --> 00:00:43,946
So, what makes the Strait of Hormuz so powerful?

20
00:00:50,926 --> 00:00:51,756
Chapter one.

21
00:00:51,776 --> 00:00:52,959
A door in the map.

22
00:00:53,659 --> 00:00:55,369
Look closely at the Persian Gulf.

23
00:00:55,449 --> 00:00:57,145
Do you notice something strange?

24
00:00:57,735 --> 00:00:58,885
It is almost a lake.

25
00:00:59,135 --> 00:01:02,085
A long, shallow sea,

26
00:01:02,105 --> 00:01:02,935
with Iran on one side and

27
00:01:02,955 --> 00:01:04,409
the Arabian Peninsula on the other.

28
00:01:05,399 --> 00:01:07,969
There is only one way out to the open ocean.

29
00:01:08,359 --> 00:01:09,209
Right here,

30
00:01:09,339 --> 00:01:13,523
where the tip of Oman's Musandam peninsula reaches toward Iran.

31
00:01:14,503 --> 00:01:16,563
That doorway is the Strait of Hormuz.

32
00:01:16,833 --> 00:01:19,653
About a hundred and sixty-seven kilometres long,

33
00:01:19,763 --> 00:01:21,183
and at its narrowest,

34
00:01:21,263 --> 00:01:24,323
only thirty-three to thirty-nine kilometres across.

35
00:01:25,543 --> 00:01:27,923
But giant tankers can't use all of that.

36
00:01:28,093 --> 00:01:29,903
They follow two marked lanes,

37
00:01:29,923 --> 00:01:32,253
each only about three kilometres wide.

38
00:01:32,663 --> 00:01:33,683
One lane in,

39
00:01:33,703 --> 00:01:34,603
one lane out,

40
00:01:34,623 --> 00:01:36,070
with a buffer between them.

41
00:01:37,250 --> 00:01:39,676
A fully loaded supertanker sits as deep in

42
00:01:39,696 --> 00:01:42,440
the water as a seven-storey building is tall,

43
00:01:42,690 --> 00:01:44,681
so it has to stay in the deep channel.

44
00:01:45,881 --> 00:01:46,471
That's it.

45
00:01:46,491 --> 00:01:48,441
Two lanes, three kilometres each.

46
00:01:48,601 --> 00:01:50,646
The world's busiest oil doorway.

47
00:01:51,756 --> 00:01:53,246
But why does so much oil need to

48
00:01:53,266 --> 00:01:55,253
pass through here in the first place?

49
00:01:56,433 --> 00:01:57,303
Chapter two.

50
00:01:57,323 --> 00:01:58,722
A fifth of the world.

51
00:01:59,432 --> 00:02:01,862
How much is actually moving through this door?

52
00:02:02,442 --> 00:02:03,697
Behind it sit some of the

53
00:02:03,717 --> 00:02:05,402
biggest oil producers on Earth.

54
00:02:05,842 --> 00:02:07,692
Saudi Arabia, Iraq,

55
00:02:07,742 --> 00:02:10,722
the United Arab Emirates, Kuwait, Iran.

56
00:02:10,912 --> 00:02:11,802
And Qatar,

57
00:02:11,872 --> 00:02:15,078
one of the world's largest exporters of natural gas.

58
00:02:16,168 --> 00:02:17,398
In twenty twenty-five,

59
00:02:17,478 --> 00:02:19,158
about twenty million barrels of oil

60
00:02:19,178 --> 00:02:21,088
and fuel passed through the strait.

61
00:02:21,388 --> 00:02:22,671
Every single day.

62
00:02:23,571 --> 00:02:26,141
That's like ten fully loaded supertankers,

63
00:02:26,251 --> 00:02:27,301
like the one we met,

64
00:02:27,351 --> 00:02:28,678
leaving every day.

65
00:02:29,278 --> 00:02:32,728
That is around two hundred and thirty barrels every second.

66
00:02:33,048 --> 00:02:36,369
Enough to fill an Olympic swimming pool roughly every minute.

67
00:02:37,759 --> 00:02:39,005
It was about a fifth of

68
00:02:39,025 --> 00:02:40,669
all the oil the world uses,

69
00:02:40,819 --> 00:02:43,515
and a quarter of all the oil traded by sea.

70
00:02:44,405 --> 00:02:47,965
Plus about a fifth of the world's liquefied natural gas.

71
00:02:48,955 --> 00:02:50,405
Most of it heads east.

72
00:02:50,635 --> 00:02:53,255
More than eighty percent of the crude goes to Asia,

73
00:02:53,365 --> 00:02:55,255
mainly to China, India,

74
00:02:55,275 --> 00:02:57,015
Japan and South Korea.

75
00:02:58,415 --> 00:02:59,946
So one doorway feeds a huge

76
00:02:59,966 --> 00:03:01,675
part of the world's engines.

77
00:03:01,865 --> 00:03:03,458
Surely there's a back door?

78
00:03:04,628 --> 00:03:05,468
Chapter three.

79
00:03:05,488 --> 00:03:06,650
The back doors.

80
00:03:07,330 --> 00:03:09,040
If the front door is so risky,

81
00:03:09,110 --> 00:03:10,686
why not just go around it?

82
00:03:11,276 --> 00:03:12,696
Some countries tried.

83
00:03:12,936 --> 00:03:16,186
Saudi Arabia built a pipeline right across the desert,

84
00:03:16,306 --> 00:03:18,116
about twelve hundred kilometres,

85
00:03:18,136 --> 00:03:19,251
to the Red Sea.

86
00:03:20,441 --> 00:03:22,582
The United Arab Emirates built one

87
00:03:22,602 --> 00:03:24,111
to the port of Fujairah,

88
00:03:24,131 --> 00:03:25,270
outside the strait.

89
00:03:25,290 --> 00:03:27,009
Iran built one too, to Jask,

90
00:03:27,029 --> 00:03:29,142
but it has barely been used.

91
00:03:30,122 --> 00:03:32,142
Add up the spare room in these pipes,

92
00:03:32,302 --> 00:03:35,725
and the International Energy Agency estimates roughly three and a

93
00:03:35,745 --> 00:03:38,641
half to five and a half million barrels a day.

94
00:03:39,531 --> 00:03:40,831
Against twenty million.

95
00:03:41,061 --> 00:03:41,771
At best,

96
00:03:41,791 --> 00:03:44,413
about one barrel in four could take the back door.

97
00:03:45,603 --> 00:03:46,310
And for gas,

98
00:03:46,330 --> 00:03:48,057
there is no back door at all.

99
00:03:48,077 --> 00:03:51,356
Almost all of Qatar's gas exports leave through the strait,

100
00:03:51,376 --> 00:03:53,471
with no other route to market.

101
00:03:54,571 --> 00:03:57,141
So the world depends on this door staying open.

102
00:03:57,371 --> 00:03:59,315
Has anyone tried to close it before?

103
00:04:00,505 --> 00:04:01,405
Chapter four.

104
00:04:01,425 --> 00:04:02,593
The Tanker War.

105
00:04:03,313 --> 00:04:05,293
Here is something many people believe:

106
00:04:05,313 --> 00:04:06,520
if the shooting starts,

107
00:04:06,540 --> 00:04:07,631
the oil stops.

108
00:04:08,211 --> 00:04:09,411
In the nineteen eighties,

109
00:04:09,431 --> 00:04:11,271
Iran and Iraq were at war.

110
00:04:11,651 --> 00:04:13,031
From nineteen eighty-four,

111
00:04:13,051 --> 00:04:15,361
both sides attacked ships in the Gulf,

112
00:04:15,421 --> 00:04:17,306
to hit each other's oil money.

113
00:04:18,286 --> 00:04:19,716
Over the next four years,

114
00:04:19,796 --> 00:04:21,936
four hundred and fifty-one ships were hit.

115
00:04:22,196 --> 00:04:24,885
More than four hundred sailors lost their lives.

116
00:04:26,365 --> 00:04:27,935
In nineteen eighty-seven,

117
00:04:28,025 --> 00:04:30,065
the tanker Bridgeton struck a mine.

118
00:04:30,415 --> 00:04:34,350
The United States began escorting Kuwaiti tankers in convoys.

119
00:04:35,550 --> 00:04:37,630
It sounds like the oil must have stopped.

120
00:04:37,740 --> 00:04:38,587
It didn't.

121
00:04:39,567 --> 00:04:41,097
Even at the worst moments,

122
00:04:41,207 --> 00:04:42,872
the attacks hit no more than about

123
00:04:42,892 --> 00:04:45,067
two percent of the ships passing through.

124
00:04:45,497 --> 00:04:47,352
Iran even cut its oil prices to

125
00:04:47,372 --> 00:04:49,579
make up for the higher insurance.

126
00:04:50,709 --> 00:04:52,439
There were short price spikes.

127
00:04:52,689 --> 00:04:53,979
But across the decade,

128
00:04:53,999 --> 00:04:55,509
prices kept sliding.

129
00:04:55,769 --> 00:04:57,379
And in nineteen eighty-six,

130
00:04:57,399 --> 00:04:59,829
for reasons that had nothing to do with the war,

131
00:04:59,889 --> 00:05:02,250
they crashed below ten dollars a barrel.

132
00:05:03,440 --> 00:05:05,620
So attacks alone didn't close the door.

133
00:05:05,730 --> 00:05:06,765
What would it take?

134
00:05:07,945 --> 00:05:08,895
Chapter five.

135
00:05:08,955 --> 00:05:10,703
The year the door almost shut.

136
00:05:11,383 --> 00:05:12,733
In twenty twenty-six,

137
00:05:12,753 --> 00:05:15,593
something happened that never happened in the Tanker War.

138
00:05:15,913 --> 00:05:18,003
The ships themselves stopped coming.

139
00:05:18,583 --> 00:05:20,033
On February the twenty-eighth,

140
00:05:20,173 --> 00:05:23,353
the United States and Israel began strikes on Iran.

141
00:05:23,733 --> 00:05:26,739
Within days, Iran declared the strait closed.

142
00:05:27,929 --> 00:05:30,229
Then came the part most people never see.

143
00:05:30,529 --> 00:05:32,709
Insurers pulled their war-risk cover.

144
00:05:32,909 --> 00:05:34,159
And without insurance,

145
00:05:34,179 --> 00:05:36,459
most ship owners simply won't sail.

146
00:05:37,349 --> 00:05:39,727
War-risk premiums jumped from a fraction of a

147
00:05:39,747 --> 00:05:42,349
percent of a ship's value to several percent,

148
00:05:42,519 --> 00:05:43,938
for a single voyage.

149
00:05:44,918 --> 00:05:45,728
Before the war,

150
00:05:45,748 --> 00:05:47,282
more than a hundred ships passed

151
00:05:47,302 --> 00:05:48,708
through on a normal day.

152
00:05:49,048 --> 00:05:49,898
By the summer,

153
00:05:49,918 --> 00:05:51,478
it was around five a day.

154
00:05:51,788 --> 00:05:54,247
A drop of about ninety-five percent.

155
00:05:55,467 --> 00:05:58,327
Brent crude, the world's benchmark oil price,

156
00:05:58,397 --> 00:06:00,467
had been around seventy dollars a barrel.

157
00:06:00,867 --> 00:06:01,897
At the end of April,

158
00:06:01,917 --> 00:06:04,821
it briefly touched about a hundred and twenty-six.

159
00:06:06,001 --> 00:06:08,561
Governments opened their emergency tanks.

160
00:06:08,901 --> 00:06:12,971
In March,

161
00:06:13,111 --> 00:06:14,831
the thirty-two members of the International Energy Agency agreed

162
00:06:14,851 --> 00:06:16,525
to release a record four hundred million barrels.

163
00:06:17,425 --> 00:06:18,925
That sounds enormous.

164
00:06:19,165 --> 00:06:21,065
But it equals only about twenty days

165
00:06:21,085 --> 00:06:23,321
of what normally flows through Hormuz.

166
00:06:24,511 --> 00:06:25,301
By the autumn,

167
00:06:25,321 --> 00:06:26,741
ships were moving again,

168
00:06:26,781 --> 00:06:29,071
but only a fraction of the old traffic.

169
00:06:29,391 --> 00:06:30,201
This week,

170
00:06:30,221 --> 00:06:33,251
Iran said several tankers hit mines in the strait,

171
00:06:33,351 --> 00:06:36,061
a claim that could not be independently verified.

172
00:06:36,411 --> 00:06:37,798
Talks have stalled.

173
00:06:39,188 --> 00:06:39,978
But wait.

174
00:06:40,138 --> 00:06:42,148
If only part of the oil went missing,

175
00:06:42,198 --> 00:06:44,347
why did prices jump so much?

176
00:06:45,537 --> 00:06:46,328
Chapter six.

177
00:06:46,348 --> 00:06:48,073
Small gap, big price.

178
00:06:48,763 --> 00:06:50,119
You don't need to lose much

179
00:06:50,139 --> 00:06:51,683
oil for prices to explode.

180
00:06:51,903 --> 00:06:52,941
Here's why.

181
00:06:53,521 --> 00:06:55,081
In nineteen seventy-nine,

182
00:06:55,171 --> 00:06:57,071
during the Iranian revolution,

183
00:06:57,141 --> 00:07:00,631
world oil supply fell by only about five percent.

184
00:07:00,941 --> 00:07:02,573
Prices more than doubled.

185
00:07:03,743 --> 00:07:04,993
The reason is simple.

186
00:07:05,203 --> 00:07:06,033
In the short run,

187
00:07:06,053 --> 00:07:08,273
almost nobody can stop using oil.

188
00:07:08,493 --> 00:07:10,033
Trucks still deliver food.

189
00:07:10,123 --> 00:07:11,503
Planes still fly.

190
00:07:11,713 --> 00:07:13,338
Farms still need diesel.

191
00:07:14,338 --> 00:07:16,038
When buyers can't cut back,

192
00:07:16,118 --> 00:07:18,868
even a small shortage turns into a bidding war.

193
00:07:19,278 --> 00:07:22,261
The price climbs until enough people are priced out.

194
00:07:23,361 --> 00:07:24,631
And the cost spreads.

195
00:07:24,881 --> 00:07:26,141
In the United States,

196
00:07:26,161 --> 00:07:27,666
crude oil makes up about half of

197
00:07:27,686 --> 00:07:29,385
what drivers pay at the pump.

198
00:07:30,465 --> 00:07:33,655
Then it rides along into everything that moves on wheels,

199
00:07:33,675 --> 00:07:35,145
wings or keels.

200
00:07:35,425 --> 00:07:36,935
Which is almost everything.

201
00:07:37,915 --> 00:07:38,885
A narrow door,

202
00:07:38,905 --> 00:07:41,495
plus a fuel nobody can quickly live without.

203
00:07:41,805 --> 00:07:43,831
That's a shock that reaches your wallet.

204
00:07:45,211 --> 00:07:48,119
So, why can one narrow strait shake the whole world?

205
00:07:48,699 --> 00:07:51,509
Because geography gave the Gulf just one door.

206
00:07:51,799 --> 00:07:52,359
Because a fifth of the world's oil,

207
00:07:52,379 --> 00:07:53,549
and a fifth of its gas,

208
00:07:53,569 --> 00:07:54,839
go through it.

209
00:07:54,859 --> 00:07:55,699
Because the back doors are small.

210
00:07:55,939 --> 00:07:59,689
And because, in the short run,

211
00:07:59,709 --> 00:08:01,741
the world can't stop using oil.

212
00:08:02,991 --> 00:08:04,049
Forty years ago,

213
00:08:04,069 --> 00:08:06,660
attacks alone could not shut that door.

214
00:08:06,680 --> 00:08:07,909
Ships kept sailing.

215
00:08:07,929 --> 00:08:09,895
This year, risk itself did it.

216
00:08:09,915 --> 00:08:11,717
No insurance, no ships.

217
00:08:12,897 --> 00:08:15,977
That supertanker from the beginning is waiting for one thing:

218
00:08:16,217 --> 00:08:18,357
the moment the door feels safe again.

219
00:08:18,627 --> 00:08:19,607
And until then,

220
00:08:19,637 --> 00:08:23,037
the whole world pays for every barrel that stays behind.

221
00:08:24,617 --> 00:08:26,367
If you enjoyed this,

222
00:08:26,387 --> 00:08:27,957
give it a like,

223
00:08:28,237 --> 00:08:29,617
and subscribe to Whyverse.

224
